1
00:00:00,275 --> 00:00:04,738
Every few months, a new AI model arrives that anyone can download, not far

2
00:00:04,738 --> 00:00:06,762
behind the best ones that you can't.

3
00:00:06,762 --> 00:00:10,212
Here's that race, as tracked by Artificial Analysis.

4
00:00:10,212 --> 00:00:14,625
Their Intelligence Index combines ten evaluations, from agentic office work

5
00:00:14,625 --> 00:00:17,725
to coding in a terminal to research-level physics.

6
00:00:17,725 --> 00:00:20,012
Grey dots are proprietary models;

7
00:00:20,012 --> 00:00:24,662
blue dots are open-weights models, whose parameters anyone can download.

8
00:00:24,662 --> 00:00:28,150
The best open model has followed the best closed one at a distance of a few

9
00:00:28,150 --> 00:00:29,442
months.

10
00:00:29,442 --> 00:00:34,367
As of October 2026, the top proprietary model scores about 58.

11
00:00:34,367 --> 00:00:37,129
And the top three open-weights models are these:

12
00:00:37,129 --> 00:00:41,792
MiMo-V2.6-Pro, from Xiaomi, at 46;

13
00:00:41,792 --> 00:00:46,304
GLM-5.3, from Z.ai, at 45;

14
00:00:46,304 --> 00:00:51,592
and Kimi K3, from Moonshot AI, at 44.

15
00:00:52,242 --> 00:00:54,554
These three are the subject of this video.

16
00:00:54,554 --> 00:00:55,879
They're enormous.

17
00:00:55,879 --> 00:00:59,504
Counting every parameter in the weight files each lab published:

18
00:00:59,504 --> 00:01:04,192
MiMo has about a trillion, GLM about three quarters of a trillion, and Kimi

19
00:01:04,192 --> 00:01:08,204
K3 2.8 trillion, by far the largest of the three.

20
00:01:08,204 --> 00:01:12,304
Yet each one uses only a small slice of itself for any one token:

21
00:01:12,304 --> 00:01:17,917
about 42 billion parameters, 41 billion, and 105 billion.

22
00:01:17,917 --> 00:01:21,617
And each one can read a million tokens of context at once:

23
00:01:21,617 --> 00:01:26,342
several thick novels, or a whole software project.

24
00:01:26,475 --> 00:01:29,225
Under the hood, all three are still transformers:

25
00:01:29,225 --> 00:01:35,050
the same basic blueprint as GPT-2, back in 2019, a tall stack of layers.

26
00:01:35,050 --> 00:01:38,525
But getting to this scale forced each team to make different bets, and they

27
00:01:38,525 --> 00:01:41,150
ended up as three quite different machines.

28
00:01:41,150 --> 00:01:45,775
MiMo makes most of its layers look only at the last 128 tokens.

29
00:01:45,775 --> 00:01:50,575
GLM squeezes each token's memory into a compressed code, and then reads only

30
00:01:50,575 --> 00:01:53,925
the two thousand or so most relevant tokens.

31
00:01:53,925 --> 00:01:58,725
And Kimi K3 replaces three quarters of its attention layers with a memory

32
00:01:58,725 --> 00:02:03,475
of fixed size, which rewrites itself as it reads.

33
00:02:03,658 --> 00:02:05,983
In this video we'll open up all three.

34
00:02:05,983 --> 00:02:07,533
First, the trick they share:

35
00:02:07,533 --> 00:02:09,196
the mixture of experts.

36
00:02:09,196 --> 00:02:12,846
Then the central problem of long context, and the three different ways they

37
00:02:12,846 --> 00:02:13,821
solve it.

38
00:02:13,821 --> 00:02:17,683
Then Kimi K3's new ideas for the depth and width of a network.

39
00:02:17,683 --> 00:02:20,346
And finally, all three side by side.

40
00:02:20,346 --> 00:02:24,421
Everything on screen that describes one of these models, from its layer layout

41
00:02:24,421 --> 00:02:28,008
to its parameter counts to a few of its learned weights, is read straight

42
00:02:28,008 --> 00:02:30,083
from the files its lab published.

43
00:02:39,465 --> 00:02:41,853
Let's start from the blueprint all three share.

44
00:02:41,853 --> 00:02:46,353
Text is cut into tokens, each token becomes a long vector of numbers, and

45
00:02:46,353 --> 00:02:49,203
those vectors flow up through a stack of layers.

46
00:02:49,203 --> 00:02:51,153
Each layer has two parts.

47
00:02:51,153 --> 00:02:55,365
In attention, every token looks back at the tokens before it, and pulls in

48
00:02:55,365 --> 00:02:57,003
whatever is relevant.

49
00:02:57,003 --> 00:03:01,203
In the feed-forward network, each token is processed on its own, by a big

50
00:03:01,203 --> 00:03:02,528
two-layer network;

51
00:03:02,528 --> 00:03:06,065
this is where much of a model's knowledge seems to be stored.

52
00:03:06,065 --> 00:03:10,090
At the top, the final vector becomes a probability for every possible next

53
00:03:10,090 --> 00:03:11,028
token.

54
00:03:11,028 --> 00:03:14,915
Pick one, append it, and repeat.

55
00:03:15,015 --> 00:03:17,890
At this scale, that design runs up two bills.

56
00:03:17,890 --> 00:03:19,578
The first is compute.

57
00:03:19,578 --> 00:03:24,115
In a plain, dense transformer, every parameter takes part in processing every

58
00:03:24,115 --> 00:03:25,053
token:

59
00:03:25,053 --> 00:03:29,865
about two arithmetic operations per parameter, per token, one multiply and

60
00:03:29,865 --> 00:03:31,003
one add.

61
00:03:31,003 --> 00:03:34,853
For a trillion-parameter model, that would be two trillion operations for

62
00:03:34,853 --> 00:03:38,965
every single token it reads or writes.

63
00:03:39,082 --> 00:03:43,219
The second bill is memory, and it grows with the length of the context.

64
00:03:43,219 --> 00:03:46,519
To avoid recomputing, attention keeps a cache:

65
00:03:46,519 --> 00:03:50,869
a key and a value vector for every past token, in every layer.

66
00:03:50,869 --> 00:03:54,744
Each new token compares its query against all of those keys.

67
00:03:54,744 --> 00:03:58,994
So the cache grows with every token you add, and over a whole text, the number

68
00:03:58,994 --> 00:04:02,365
of comparisons grows with the square of its length.

69
00:04:02,365 --> 00:04:06,965
All three models pay the first bill the same way, with a mixture of experts.

70
00:04:06,965 --> 00:04:09,165
The second bill is where they part ways.

71
00:04:13,405 --> 00:04:16,218
Here's the idea behind a mixture of experts.

72
00:04:16,218 --> 00:04:20,093
Take the big feed-forward network inside one layer, and split it into many

73
00:04:20,093 --> 00:04:22,355
small ones, called experts.

74
00:04:22,355 --> 00:04:26,530
Then add a small router, which looks at each token and picks just a few experts

75
00:04:26,530 --> 00:04:27,318
for it.

76
00:04:27,318 --> 00:04:29,193
Only those experts run.

77
00:04:29,193 --> 00:04:33,093
Their outputs are blended, with weights from the router, and added back into

78
00:04:33,093 --> 00:04:34,593
the token's vector.

79
00:04:34,593 --> 00:04:37,939
The other experts sit idle, for this token.

80
00:04:37,939 --> 00:04:41,401
This breaks the link between the two halves of the first bill.

81
00:04:41,401 --> 00:04:45,876
The model's capacity to store knowledge grows with all of its experts, while

82
00:04:45,876 --> 00:04:52,114
the compute for each token depends only on the few experts that actually run.

83
00:04:52,272 --> 00:04:53,835
Here are the real numbers.

84
00:04:53,835 --> 00:04:59,872
In each expert layer, MiMo has 384 experts, and every token uses 8 of them.

85
00:04:59,872 --> 00:05:05,797
GLM has 256, and also uses 8, plus one shared expert that every token passes

86
00:05:05,797 --> 00:05:06,547
through.

87
00:05:06,547 --> 00:05:12,997
And Kimi K3 has 896 experts, uses 16 of them, plus two shared experts.

88
00:05:12,997 --> 00:05:17,597
Nearly every layer of each model has a block like this.

89
00:05:17,789 --> 00:05:21,564
Now let's count every parameter in each model, using the shapes of all the

90
00:05:21,564 --> 00:05:24,026
tensors in the published weight files.

91
00:05:24,026 --> 00:05:26,926
The routed experts are almost the entire model:

92
00:05:26,926 --> 00:05:33,564
98 percent of MiMo, 96 percent of GLM, and 98 percent of Kimi K3.

93
00:05:33,564 --> 00:05:37,451
Attention, the embeddings, the router, the shared experts:

94
00:05:37,451 --> 00:05:42,714
all of it is a thin shell around a vast library of experts.

95
00:05:42,872 --> 00:05:45,922
Why so many small experts, instead of a few big ones?

96
00:05:45,922 --> 00:05:48,222
Because what matters is the combination.

97
00:05:48,222 --> 00:05:52,697
With 8 big experts and 2 picks, there are only 28 possible teams.

98
00:05:52,697 --> 00:05:56,635
With 8 picks out of 384, there are about ten quadrillion.

99
00:05:56,635 --> 00:06:01,147
Small, specialized experts can be mixed and matched far more flexibly.

100
00:06:01,147 --> 00:06:05,072
The shared experts in GLM and Kimi play the opposite role:

101
00:06:05,072 --> 00:06:08,822
they handle whatever every token needs, so the routed experts are free to

102
00:06:08,822 --> 00:06:09,772
specialize.

103
00:06:14,004 --> 00:06:15,554
How does the router choose?

104
00:06:15,554 --> 00:06:18,641
Each expert has a learned vector, a bit like a key.

105
00:06:18,641 --> 00:06:22,079
The router takes the dot product of the token's vector with every expert's

106
00:06:22,079 --> 00:06:26,266
vector, and squashes each result into a score between zero and one, with a

107
00:06:26,266 --> 00:06:27,254
sigmoid.

108
00:06:27,254 --> 00:06:28,516
The top scores win:

109
00:06:28,516 --> 00:06:32,241
here two out of eight, in MiMo eight out of 384.

110
00:06:32,241 --> 00:06:36,391
Their scores, rescaled to add up to one, become the blending weights.

111
00:06:36,391 --> 00:06:40,279
All three models use this same recipe.

112
00:06:40,379 --> 00:06:44,041
But left alone, this goes wrong in a predictable way.

113
00:06:44,041 --> 00:06:48,091
An expert that gets chosen a lot gets more training, so it gets better, so

114
00:06:48,091 --> 00:06:50,041
it gets chosen even more.

115
00:06:50,041 --> 00:06:54,416
A few experts end up overloaded, and others are starved, and never learn much

116
00:06:54,416 --> 00:06:55,479
of anything.

117
00:06:55,479 --> 00:07:00,116
And since the experts are spread across many GPUs, an overloaded expert means

118
00:07:00,116 --> 00:07:04,479
one GPU working flat out while the others wait.

119
00:07:04,612 --> 00:07:08,500
The classic fix was an extra penalty in the training loss for imbalance, but

120
00:07:08,500 --> 00:07:12,100
that penalty fights against the real goal, predicting text well.

121
00:07:12,100 --> 00:07:15,750
All three models instead use a trick introduced by DeepSeek:

122
00:07:15,750 --> 00:07:19,825
give each expert a bias, a kind of handicap, that is added to its score only

123
00:07:19,825 --> 00:07:23,012
when choosing the winners, not when blending their outputs.

124
00:07:23,012 --> 00:07:25,787
If an expert is overloaded, lower its bias;

125
00:07:25,787 --> 00:07:27,775
if it's underused, raise it.

126
00:07:27,775 --> 00:07:29,412
The router learns freely;

127
00:07:29,412 --> 00:07:33,062
the biases just keep the traffic fair.

128
00:07:33,229 --> 00:07:37,704
These are the actual balancing biases of one layer in each model, read straight

129
00:07:37,704 --> 00:07:45,929
from the weight files, MiMo's 384, GLM's 256, and Kimi's 896, sorted from

130
00:07:45,929 --> 00:07:47,766
largest to smallest.

131
00:07:47,766 --> 00:07:52,404
Each one records how much the balancing had to boost or hold back one expert.

132
00:07:52,404 --> 00:07:55,879
Notice that GLM's are all around eight.

133
00:07:55,879 --> 00:07:56,791
That's fine:

134
00:07:56,791 --> 00:08:00,679
only the differences between experts matter, since adding the same number

135
00:08:00,679 --> 00:08:05,629
to every score never changes which ones come out on top.

136
00:08:05,770 --> 00:08:07,895
Kimi K3 sharpens this.

137
00:08:07,895 --> 00:08:13,733
With 896 experts, small fixed nudges are too slow, and big ones overshoot.

138
00:08:13,733 --> 00:08:17,895
So instead of nudging, its Quantile Balancing computes, from a single batch,

139
00:08:17,895 --> 00:08:21,633
the bias that would give each expert exactly its fair share.

140
00:08:21,633 --> 00:08:26,033
Take one expert, and for every token in the batch, measure how far that token's

141
00:08:26,033 --> 00:08:29,520
score for this expert is from the cutoff it would need to make the token's

142
00:08:29,520 --> 00:08:31,170
top sixteen.

143
00:08:31,170 --> 00:08:34,683
Then put the bias at the point where exactly the fair number of tokens get

144
00:08:34,683 --> 00:08:35,458
through:

145
00:08:35,458 --> 00:08:36,970
a quantile.

146
00:08:36,970 --> 00:08:41,145
Here's a toy run with 16 experts, on random tokens.

147
00:08:41,145 --> 00:08:45,920
With fixed nudges, the busiest expert starts at more than twice its fair share,

148
00:08:45,920 --> 00:08:48,508
and it takes about ten steps to come down.

149
00:08:48,508 --> 00:08:51,470
The quantile rule gets most of the way in a single step.

150
00:08:55,727 --> 00:08:57,839
Let's put numbers on the second bill.

151
00:08:57,839 --> 00:09:02,952
Suppose GLM-5.3 used the textbook version of attention, where each of its

152
00:09:02,952 --> 00:09:08,639
64 heads stores a key and a value of 256 numbers, for every token, in each

153
00:09:08,639 --> 00:09:10,489
of its 78 layers.

154
00:09:10,489 --> 00:09:12,977
That's over 5 megabytes per token.

155
00:09:12,977 --> 00:09:16,364
At a million tokens of context, 5 terabytes:

156
00:09:16,364 --> 00:09:19,602
about seven times the size of the model's own weights.

157
00:09:19,602 --> 00:09:23,877
And every new token would have to read through all of it.

158
00:09:24,002 --> 00:09:26,364
And compute grows even faster.

159
00:09:26,364 --> 00:09:30,927
Across a whole document, attention compares every token with every token before

160
00:09:30,927 --> 00:09:31,614
it.

161
00:09:31,614 --> 00:09:35,889
For a million tokens, that's about half a trillion pairs, in every head of

162
00:09:35,889 --> 00:09:38,427
every layer.

163
00:09:38,568 --> 00:09:41,593
The three models answer this in three different ways.

164
00:09:41,593 --> 00:09:43,081
MiMo looks nearby:

165
00:09:43,081 --> 00:09:46,718
most of its layers only see a short window of recent tokens.

166
00:09:46,718 --> 00:09:51,331
GLM compresses each token's memory into a small code, and then reads only

167
00:09:51,331 --> 00:09:52,993
the entries that matter.

168
00:09:52,993 --> 00:09:57,493
Kimi keeps a running summary of fixed size, which it rewrites as it reads.

169
00:09:57,493 --> 00:10:01,806
And all three keep some layers of full, global attention, as a lifeline to

170
00:10:01,806 --> 00:10:03,206
the distant past.

171
00:10:03,206 --> 00:10:04,918
Let's take them one at a time.

172
00:10:09,193 --> 00:10:10,380
MiMo first.

173
00:10:10,380 --> 00:10:12,955
Here's the pattern of a normal attention layer:

174
00:10:12,955 --> 00:10:16,880
each row is a token, and each column a token it can look at.

175
00:10:16,880 --> 00:10:21,180
Every token sees everything before it, so the pattern is a triangle.

176
00:10:21,180 --> 00:10:26,630
In a sliding-window layer, each token only sees the most recent 128 tokens,

177
00:10:26,630 --> 00:10:28,155
itself included.

178
00:10:28,155 --> 00:10:32,568
The triangle becomes a narrow band, and the band never gets wider, however

179
00:10:32,568 --> 00:10:35,068
long the text.

180
00:10:35,176 --> 00:10:40,239
Of MiMo's 70 layers, 60 are sliding-window layers, and 10 are global.

181
00:10:40,239 --> 00:10:41,826
Here's the real layout.

182
00:10:41,826 --> 00:10:46,126
The first layer is global, and then a global layer comes after every six or

183
00:10:46,126 --> 00:10:48,639
seven local ones, all the way up.

184
00:10:48,639 --> 00:10:52,526
The very last layer is global too.

185
00:10:52,668 --> 00:10:54,993
Information can still travel far.

186
00:10:54,993 --> 00:10:58,968
Within one local layer, a token can only pull from its window.

187
00:10:58,968 --> 00:11:02,193
But the next layer up can pull from tokens that have already pulled from their

188
00:11:02,193 --> 00:11:05,530
windows, so the reach grows with every layer.

189
00:11:05,530 --> 00:11:11,743
Through MiMo's 60 local layers, information could creep back about 7,600 tokens.

190
00:11:11,743 --> 00:11:14,518
But the long jumps are the global layers' job:

191
00:11:14,518 --> 00:11:19,168
they reach any token in the context, in a single hop.

192
00:11:19,276 --> 00:11:21,014
The payoff is memory.

193
00:11:21,014 --> 00:11:26,101
A local layer only has to cache its last 128 tokens, no matter how long the

194
00:11:26,101 --> 00:11:27,589
context gets.

195
00:11:27,589 --> 00:11:30,714
Only the 10 global layers have to keep everything.

196
00:11:30,714 --> 00:11:35,901
So per token of context, MiMo caches about 51 kilobytes, where the same model

197
00:11:35,901 --> 00:11:39,351
with every layer global would need 358.

198
00:11:39,351 --> 00:11:45,601
At a million tokens, that's 51 gigabytes instead of 358.

199
00:11:45,784 --> 00:11:49,809
MiMo shrinks the cache in a second way, too, called grouped-query attention.

200
00:11:49,809 --> 00:11:55,072
It has 128 query heads, but they share just 8 sets of cached keys and values:

201
00:11:55,072 --> 00:11:57,184
sixteen heads read from each one.

202
00:11:57,184 --> 00:12:02,122
And within each head, only the first 64 of the 192 query and key dimensions

203
00:12:02,122 --> 00:12:04,422
are rotated to encode position.

204
00:12:04,422 --> 00:12:06,784
The rest match on content alone.

205
00:12:06,784 --> 00:12:11,584
The global layers rotate slowly, suited to distances of up to a million tokens,

206
00:12:11,584 --> 00:12:13,584
and the local layers rotate fast.

207
00:12:17,808 --> 00:12:21,720
But a window this small creates a problem, and to see it, we have to look

208
00:12:21,720 --> 00:12:23,670
closely at the softmax.

209
00:12:23,670 --> 00:12:27,545
Attention turns a head's scores into weights, and those weights always add

210
00:12:27,545 --> 00:12:29,133
up to exactly one.

211
00:12:29,133 --> 00:12:32,383
So what should a head do when nothing in its window is relevant?

212
00:12:32,383 --> 00:12:34,095
It can't attend to nothing.

213
00:12:34,095 --> 00:12:37,870
The weights still have to go somewhere, so the head ends up mixing in a little

214
00:12:37,870 --> 00:12:39,033
of everything:

215
00:12:39,033 --> 00:12:40,433
noise.

216
00:12:40,433 --> 00:12:43,358
Full-attention models found their own workaround.

217
00:12:43,358 --> 00:12:47,333
Many heads dump their unwanted attention onto the very first token of the

218
00:12:47,333 --> 00:12:50,233
text, a so-called attention sink.

219
00:12:50,233 --> 00:12:55,308
But in a sliding window, the first token is long gone.

220
00:12:55,483 --> 00:13:00,333
MiMo's fix, borrowed from OpenAI's gpt-oss models, is to give every head a

221
00:13:00,333 --> 00:13:01,595
learnable sink:

222
00:13:01,595 --> 00:13:06,508
one extra score, with no token behind it, that sits in the softmax's denominator,

223
00:13:06,508 --> 00:13:09,208
like a phantom token with a learned score.

224
00:13:09,208 --> 00:13:13,183
When the real scores are unremarkable, the sink soaks up much of the weight,

225
00:13:13,183 --> 00:13:15,470
and that weight is simply thrown away.

226
00:13:15,470 --> 00:13:18,558
The head's real weights now add up to less than one.

227
00:13:18,558 --> 00:13:20,866
It's allowed to look at nothing.

228
00:13:20,866 --> 00:13:25,504
And when one token really does matter, its score beats the sink easily, and

229
00:13:25,504 --> 00:13:29,991
the head attends to it almost as if the sink weren't there.

230
00:13:30,183 --> 00:13:33,020
These are MiMo's actual learned sink scores:

231
00:13:33,020 --> 00:13:38,095
one for each of the 128 heads, in each of the 60 sliding-window layers.

232
00:13:38,095 --> 00:13:41,995
Almost all of them are positive, typically between about a half and one, so

233
00:13:41,995 --> 00:13:46,120
the phantom token is a serious competitor in nearly every head.

234
00:13:46,120 --> 00:13:50,670
In the deepest layers, a few heads have learned strongly negative values:

235
00:13:50,670 --> 00:13:54,833
for them, the sink almost never wins.

236
00:13:54,941 --> 00:13:59,004
With sinks in place, Xiaomi found something surprising, in experiments on

237
00:13:59,004 --> 00:14:01,941
a 32-billion-parameter test model.

238
00:14:01,941 --> 00:14:05,641
Without sinks, a 128-token window hurt.

239
00:14:05,641 --> 00:14:10,441
With sinks, it matched or beat a model with global attention in every layer.

240
00:14:10,441 --> 00:14:16,116
And after training on long documents, the 128-token window beat a 512-token

241
00:14:16,116 --> 00:14:20,291
window on long-context tests, by a wide margin.

242
00:14:20,291 --> 00:14:23,454
Xiaomi's explanation is a division of labor:

243
00:14:23,454 --> 00:14:28,191
a small window forces the local layers to handle only local structure, and

244
00:14:28,191 --> 00:14:31,404
leaves everything far away to the global layers.

245
00:14:31,404 --> 00:14:33,591
A bigger window blurs that line.

246
00:14:37,881 --> 00:14:40,006
GLM takes a different route.

247
00:14:40,006 --> 00:14:43,818
Its attention uses a design DeepSeek introduced, called multi-head latent

248
00:14:43,818 --> 00:14:45,343
attention, or MLA.

249
00:14:45,343 --> 00:14:46,393
The idea:

250
00:14:46,393 --> 00:14:49,093
don't cache each head's keys and values at all.

251
00:14:49,093 --> 00:14:53,331
Instead, squeeze the token's vector down into one small latent vector, just

252
00:14:53,331 --> 00:14:56,131
512 numbers, and cache only that.

253
00:14:56,131 --> 00:14:59,868
When a head needs a key and a value, it rebuilds them from the latent, with

254
00:14:59,868 --> 00:15:02,493
its own pair of up-projection matrices.

255
00:15:02,493 --> 00:15:06,406
One small cached vector serves all 64 heads.

256
00:15:06,406 --> 00:15:08,531
Position needs special care.

257
00:15:08,531 --> 00:15:13,043
Rotary position encoding doesn't play well with this compression, so 64 extra

258
00:15:13,043 --> 00:15:18,093
numbers carry a separate, position-rotated key, shared by all the heads.

259
00:15:18,093 --> 00:15:26,268
In total, 576 numbers per token per layer, versus 32,768 if all 64 heads cached

260
00:15:26,268 --> 00:15:28,143
full keys and values:

261
00:15:28,143 --> 00:15:31,681
57 times less memory.

262
00:15:31,831 --> 00:15:34,543
And there's an elegant trick that makes this cheap.

263
00:15:34,543 --> 00:15:38,756
A head's score for a past token is its query dotted with its rebuilt key,

264
00:15:38,756 --> 00:15:41,881
and that key is a matrix times the cached latent.

265
00:15:41,881 --> 00:15:46,606
But a dot product lets you move the matrix to the other side, onto the query.

266
00:15:46,606 --> 00:15:50,968
So each head transforms its query once, and then compares it directly against

267
00:15:50,968 --> 00:15:55,531
the cached latents, without ever decompressing them.

268
00:15:55,714 --> 00:15:58,614
GLM made two adjustments of its own.

269
00:15:58,614 --> 00:16:03,489
First, the team found that MLA, trained with the Muon optimizer, did worse

270
00:16:03,489 --> 00:16:07,564
than ordinary grouped-query attention, until they orthogonalized each head's

271
00:16:07,564 --> 00:16:10,452
projection matrix separately in the optimizer.

272
00:16:10,452 --> 00:16:11,989
That closed the gap.

273
00:16:11,989 --> 00:16:14,752
Second, it uses fewer, bigger heads:

274
00:16:14,752 --> 00:16:21,114
64 heads of 256 dimensions, instead of 96 heads of 192.

275
00:16:21,114 --> 00:16:25,202
That keeps the parameter count and the training compute the same, while cutting

276
00:16:25,202 --> 00:16:28,639
the work at each decoding step.

277
00:16:28,773 --> 00:16:32,460
But MLA shrinks what each token costs to remember;

278
00:16:32,460 --> 00:16:35,073
it doesn't change how many tokens there are.

279
00:16:35,073 --> 00:16:39,485
At a million tokens, each new token still compares itself against a million

280
00:16:39,485 --> 00:16:41,798
cached latents, in every layer.

281
00:16:46,054 --> 00:16:50,079
So GLM adds a second idea, DeepSeek Sparse Attention.

282
00:16:50,079 --> 00:16:53,779
Before the real attention runs, a tiny scorer called the lightning indexer

283
00:16:53,779 --> 00:16:57,767
looks at every earlier token, and gives each one a relevance score.

284
00:16:57,767 --> 00:17:02,729
Then each token keeps just its top 2,048, and the real attention runs only

285
00:17:02,729 --> 00:17:03,967
over those.

286
00:17:03,967 --> 00:17:09,192
Unlike a sliding window, which decides by position, this decides by content:

287
00:17:09,192 --> 00:17:13,092
a token from the very beginning of a long document can still be picked, if

288
00:17:13,092 --> 00:17:15,454
it matters.

289
00:17:15,571 --> 00:17:18,571
The indexer is like attention's cheap little cousin.

290
00:17:18,571 --> 00:17:23,233
It has 32 small heads, of 128 dimensions each.

291
00:17:23,233 --> 00:17:27,708
Each head compares the current token's indexer query with a past token's indexer

292
00:17:27,708 --> 00:17:31,921
key, keeps only the positive part, and the heads' results are added up with

293
00:17:31,921 --> 00:17:33,246
learned weights.

294
00:17:33,246 --> 00:17:38,271
It runs in 8-bit arithmetic, and needs only a small cache of its own.

295
00:17:38,271 --> 00:17:39,346
The savings:

296
00:17:39,346 --> 00:17:44,858
with a million tokens of context, the real attention reads 2,048 entries instead

297
00:17:44,858 --> 00:17:48,183
of a million, about five hundred times fewer.

298
00:17:48,183 --> 00:17:52,608
The indexer still has to scan everything, but each of its comparisons is far

299
00:17:52,608 --> 00:17:54,821
cheaper.

300
00:17:55,013 --> 00:18:01,838
GLM-5.2, the base model GLM-5.3 is built on, noticed something about the indexer's

301
00:18:01,838 --> 00:18:02,963
choices.

302
00:18:02,963 --> 00:18:05,875
Neighboring layers pick almost the same tokens.

303
00:18:05,875 --> 00:18:11,138
In Z.ai's measurements on a smaller model, adjacent layers shared 70 to 100

304
00:18:11,138 --> 00:18:12,938
percent of their selections.

305
00:18:12,938 --> 00:18:19,313
So in GLM-5.3, only about one layer in four runs the indexer, and the layers

306
00:18:19,313 --> 00:18:21,713
above it reuse its choices.

307
00:18:21,713 --> 00:18:23,250
Here's the real layout:

308
00:18:23,250 --> 00:18:27,225
21 of the 78 layers have an indexer of their own.

309
00:18:27,225 --> 00:18:33,113
Z.ai reports that this cuts the compute per token by 2.9 times, at a million

310
00:18:33,113 --> 00:18:36,063
tokens of context.

311
00:18:36,196 --> 00:18:38,058
How do you train a scorer like this?

312
00:18:38,058 --> 00:18:39,521
Not from scratch.

313
00:18:39,521 --> 00:18:44,171
GLM-5 started from a model with ordinary dense attention, first trained the

314
00:18:44,171 --> 00:18:48,283
indexer alone, to imitate where dense attention was looking, and then let

315
00:18:48,283 --> 00:18:51,083
the whole model adapt to reading sparsely.

316
00:18:51,083 --> 00:18:56,458
On long-context tests at 128 thousand tokens, the sparse model came out about

317
00:18:56,458 --> 00:18:58,058
even with the dense one:

318
00:18:58,058 --> 00:19:00,971
a little better on some, a little worse on others.

319
00:19:05,228 --> 00:19:08,041
Kimi K3 takes the most radical route.

320
00:19:08,041 --> 00:19:12,491
In 69 of its 93 layers, there's no cache of past tokens at all.

321
00:19:12,491 --> 00:19:17,153
Instead, each head keeps a single matrix of fixed size, a memory, which it

322
00:19:17,153 --> 00:19:19,941
updates as each token streams past.

323
00:19:19,941 --> 00:19:23,653
To see how that can possibly work, let's build it from scratch.

324
00:19:23,653 --> 00:19:26,116
Start with what attention does for one query:

325
00:19:26,116 --> 00:19:30,878
weigh every value by how well its key matches the query, and add them up.

326
00:19:30,878 --> 00:19:35,291
Now drop the softmax, and use the raw dot products as the weights.

327
00:19:35,291 --> 00:19:39,366
Each dot product can be written as a row times a column, and then the whole

328
00:19:39,366 --> 00:19:44,041
sum regroups into a single matrix, the sum over every token of its value times

329
00:19:44,041 --> 00:19:47,428
its key, an outer product, applied to the query.

330
00:19:47,428 --> 00:19:51,353
That matrix, S, is the memory.

331
00:19:51,545 --> 00:19:55,607
Writing a token into memory means adding its value times its key:

332
00:19:55,607 --> 00:19:59,420
a column times a row, which makes a whole grid of products.

333
00:19:59,420 --> 00:20:02,520
Reading means multiplying the memory by a query.

334
00:20:02,520 --> 00:20:06,570
And no matter how many tokens go in, S stays the same size:

335
00:20:06,570 --> 00:20:12,195
for Kimi's heads, 128 by 128 numbers.

336
00:20:12,328 --> 00:20:15,391
Think of S as a lookup table built out of geometry.

337
00:20:15,391 --> 00:20:17,216
Here it is in two dimensions.

338
00:20:17,216 --> 00:20:19,741
Store a key pointing this way, with this value.

339
00:20:19,741 --> 00:20:23,478
Read with the same key, and out comes exactly that value.

340
00:20:23,478 --> 00:20:25,141
Now store a second pair.

341
00:20:25,141 --> 00:20:28,853
Reading the first key again gives the first value, plus a little of the second,

342
00:20:28,853 --> 00:20:31,416
because the two keys aren't perpendicular.

343
00:20:31,416 --> 00:20:36,378
In 128 dimensions there's room for many nearly perpendicular keys, but every

344
00:20:36,378 --> 00:20:40,562
association you add leaves a small smudge on all the others.

345
00:20:40,562 --> 00:20:42,149
And there's a worse problem.

346
00:20:42,149 --> 00:20:43,912
Suppose a fact changes:

347
00:20:43,912 --> 00:20:48,812
the same key gets a new value, like a variable in a program being reassigned.

348
00:20:48,812 --> 00:20:52,512
The plain sum just piles the new value on top of the old one.

349
00:20:52,512 --> 00:20:55,437
Reading it back gives a blend of old and new.

350
00:20:59,668 --> 00:21:03,693
The fix is called the delta rule, and it's an old idea, from adaptive filters

351
00:21:03,693 --> 00:21:05,118
in 1960.

352
00:21:05,118 --> 00:21:06,656
Before writing, read:

353
00:21:06,656 --> 00:21:09,481
ask the memory what it currently returns for this key.

354
00:21:09,481 --> 00:21:11,743
Compare that with the value you want to store.

355
00:21:11,743 --> 00:21:13,906
And write only the difference, the error.

356
00:21:13,906 --> 00:21:18,043
This is exactly one step of gradient descent on the memory's recall error:

357
00:21:18,043 --> 00:21:21,568
the memory is learning, as it reads, to map keys to values.

358
00:21:21,568 --> 00:21:23,581
And now the reassignment works.

359
00:21:23,581 --> 00:21:28,643
The old value along that key is replaced, not piled on.

360
00:21:28,793 --> 00:21:30,256
Here's a bigger test:

361
00:21:30,256 --> 00:21:35,218
a 64-dimensional memory, and 16 keys that keep getting reassigned new random

362
00:21:35,218 --> 00:21:36,293
values.

363
00:21:36,293 --> 00:21:40,868
With the plain sum, recall of the latest values fades as the history piles

364
00:21:40,868 --> 00:21:41,643
up.

365
00:21:41,643 --> 00:21:47,168
With the delta rule, it stays high, however long the stream runs.

366
00:21:47,302 --> 00:21:50,702
Kimi Delta Attention, KDA, adds one more ingredient:

367
00:21:50,702 --> 00:21:51,577
forgetting.

368
00:21:51,577 --> 00:21:55,102
Before each write, every column of the memory is multiplied by a retention

369
00:21:55,102 --> 00:21:57,289
factor between zero and one.

370
00:21:57,289 --> 00:22:00,527
Old associations fade, making room for new ones.

371
00:22:00,527 --> 00:22:02,827
And here's KDA's refinement:

372
00:22:02,827 --> 00:22:07,089
the retention is different for each of the 128 key channels, and it's chosen

373
00:22:07,089 --> 00:22:10,089
fresh for every token, from the token itself.

374
00:22:10,089 --> 00:22:14,139
So some channels can hold on for thousands of tokens, while others turn over

375
00:22:14,139 --> 00:22:17,052
almost immediately.

376
00:22:17,168 --> 00:22:19,981
We can see this in Kimi K3's weights.

377
00:22:19,981 --> 00:22:23,706
Each channel has a learned bias that sets its retention when nothing in particular

378
00:22:23,706 --> 00:22:27,331
is happening, before each token's own input nudges it.

379
00:22:27,331 --> 00:22:31,193
Turning those into half-lives, how many tokens it takes for a stored association

380
00:22:31,193 --> 00:22:36,893
to fade to half strength, gives this spread, across all 848 thousand channels:

381
00:22:36,893 --> 00:22:40,006
from under one token, to more than ten thousand.

382
00:22:40,006 --> 00:22:42,493
The median is about 29 tokens.

383
00:22:42,493 --> 00:22:44,668
And there's a trend with depth.

384
00:22:44,668 --> 00:22:49,093
In the first KDA layer, the typical channel's memory halves within about four

385
00:22:49,093 --> 00:22:50,268
tokens.

386
00:22:50,268 --> 00:22:54,868
Deeper in the network, typical half-lives grow to tens of tokens, and in some

387
00:22:54,868 --> 00:22:57,118
layers past a hundred.

388
00:22:57,118 --> 00:22:59,056
One engineering detail:

389
00:22:59,056 --> 00:23:04,143
K3 caps how fast a channel can forget, so that no retention factor drops below

390
00:23:04,143 --> 00:23:06,956
about e to the minus five per token.

391
00:23:06,956 --> 00:23:11,768
That keeps the numbers in a range that a GPU's fast matrix units can handle,

392
00:23:11,768 --> 00:23:14,493
when tokens are processed in parallel chunks.

393
00:23:18,725 --> 00:23:21,713
A memory of fixed size has a limit, though.

394
00:23:21,713 --> 00:23:28,350
128 by 128 numbers per head can't hold an exact copy of a million tokens.

395
00:23:28,350 --> 00:23:33,375
Ask for a phone number that appeared 600,000 tokens ago, and a running summary

396
00:23:33,375 --> 00:23:36,300
may well have smudged it.

397
00:23:36,459 --> 00:23:39,346
So Kimi K3 keeps some real attention.

398
00:23:39,346 --> 00:23:43,709
Every fourth layer is a full, global attention layer, with one extra at the

399
00:23:43,709 --> 00:23:47,396
very top, so the last layer always sees everything.

400
00:23:47,396 --> 00:23:51,546
That makes 69 KDA layers and 24 global ones.

401
00:23:51,546 --> 00:23:57,984
The global layers use the same latent attention as GLM, MLA.

402
00:23:58,059 --> 00:24:00,121
Those global layers have one twist:

403
00:24:00,121 --> 00:24:02,071
no position encoding at all.

404
00:24:02,071 --> 00:24:04,446
No rotations, no position vectors.

405
00:24:04,446 --> 00:24:06,534
How can attention know word order, then?

406
00:24:06,534 --> 00:24:07,846
It doesn't have to.

407
00:24:07,846 --> 00:24:11,434
The KDA layers below already mix in order and recency:

408
00:24:11,434 --> 00:24:14,684
through their fading memories, and through a tiny convolution that blends

409
00:24:14,684 --> 00:24:16,746
each token with the three before it.

410
00:24:16,746 --> 00:24:17,884
A bonus:

411
00:24:17,884 --> 00:24:21,909
when Moonshot stretched the context during training, from 8 thousand tokens

412
00:24:21,909 --> 00:24:26,009
to 64 thousand, and later to a million, there were no position settings to

413
00:24:26,009 --> 00:24:28,309
retune.

414
00:24:28,459 --> 00:24:31,221
Both kinds of layer also end with a gate:

415
00:24:31,221 --> 00:24:35,696
each output channel is multiplied by a sigmoid computed from the token, so

416
00:24:35,696 --> 00:24:39,359
each token decides which channels of the result to let through.

417
00:24:39,359 --> 00:24:41,096
Now the memory bill.

418
00:24:41,096 --> 00:24:46,371
K3's 24 global layers cache 576 numbers per token each:

419
00:24:46,371 --> 00:24:51,909
about 28 kilobytes per token, or 28 gigabytes at a million tokens.

420
00:24:51,909 --> 00:24:57,909
The 69 KDA memories add a fixed 434 megabytes, whether the context is ten

421
00:24:57,909 --> 00:25:00,096
tokens long or a million.

422
00:25:00,096 --> 00:25:04,934
In Moonshot's earlier, smaller Kimi Linear model, this design cut the cache

423
00:25:04,934 --> 00:25:10,034
by up to 75 percent, and sped up decoding at a million tokens by up to six

424
00:25:10,034 --> 00:25:13,609
times, compared with global attention in every layer.

425
00:25:17,857 --> 00:25:21,870
Kimi's next idea applies attention in a direction you might not expect:

426
00:25:21,870 --> 00:25:23,045
across depth.

427
00:25:23,045 --> 00:25:24,995
Recall the residual stream.

428
00:25:24,995 --> 00:25:29,082
Each layer reads the running vector, computes something, and adds its result

429
00:25:29,082 --> 00:25:29,920
back.

430
00:25:29,920 --> 00:25:33,732
So the input to any layer is the embedding plus the outputs of all the layers

431
00:25:33,732 --> 00:25:36,832
below it, all added up with equal weight.

432
00:25:36,832 --> 00:25:40,657
Moonshot points out that this is much like how an old recurrent network treats

433
00:25:40,657 --> 00:25:41,470
time:

434
00:25:41,470 --> 00:25:46,332
everything that has happened gets squeezed into one running state.

435
00:25:46,432 --> 00:25:50,670
Transformers fixed that problem for sequences, by letting every token attend

436
00:25:50,670 --> 00:25:52,720
over all the tokens before it.

437
00:25:52,720 --> 00:25:55,695
Attention Residuals do the same thing for layers.

438
00:25:55,695 --> 00:25:59,632
Turn the depth axis on its side, so the earlier layers' outputs line up like

439
00:25:59,632 --> 00:26:01,320
tokens in a sequence.

440
00:26:01,320 --> 00:26:04,645
Each layer now attends over them, and chooses what to read.

441
00:26:04,645 --> 00:26:08,620
Concretely, each layer has its own learned query vector.

442
00:26:08,620 --> 00:26:12,707
The keys are the earlier outputs themselves, normalized.

443
00:26:12,707 --> 00:26:17,157
A softmax turns the matches into weights, and the layer's input is the weighted

444
00:26:17,157 --> 00:26:20,782
blend, instead of the plain sum.

445
00:26:20,899 --> 00:26:25,312
Keeping every one of 93 layers' outputs around would cost a lot of memory,

446
00:26:25,312 --> 00:26:28,587
so K3 groups its layers into blocks of 12.

447
00:26:28,587 --> 00:26:31,862
Inside a block, outputs are summed as usual.

448
00:26:31,862 --> 00:26:35,999
Across blocks, each layer attends over at most nine summaries:

449
00:26:35,999 --> 00:26:41,824
the embedding, one for each finished block, and the current block so far.

450
00:26:41,957 --> 00:26:43,770
Here are the real learned queries:

451
00:26:43,770 --> 00:26:47,870
186 of them, one for the attention half and one for the feed-forward half

452
00:26:47,870 --> 00:26:49,195
of every layer.

453
00:26:49,195 --> 00:26:52,245
This grid shows how similar each pair of queries is:

454
00:26:52,245 --> 00:26:55,220
brighter means they point in more similar directions.

455
00:26:55,220 --> 00:26:58,820
The dashed lines are the block boundaries, and the bright squares line up

456
00:26:58,820 --> 00:26:59,682
with them.

457
00:26:59,682 --> 00:27:04,270
Neighbouring layers in the same block have similarity of about 0.47;

458
00:27:04,270 --> 00:27:08,582
neighbours across a boundary, only about 0.25.

459
00:27:08,582 --> 00:27:12,707
Layers in the same block see the same set of past summaries, and they learned

460
00:27:12,707 --> 00:27:14,657
to ask similar questions of them.

461
00:27:18,914 --> 00:27:22,727
Finally, Kimi pushes the mixture of experts further than the other two:

462
00:27:22,727 --> 00:27:27,002
896 experts per layer, 16 for each token.

463
00:27:27,002 --> 00:27:29,002
That creates a traffic problem.

464
00:27:29,002 --> 00:27:33,464
In a big deployment, the experts live on different GPUs, so every token's

465
00:27:33,464 --> 00:27:37,564
vector has to be shipped to each of its 16 experts, and the results shipped

466
00:27:37,564 --> 00:27:38,489
back:

467
00:27:38,489 --> 00:27:45,364
16 copies of a 7,168-number vector, per token, per layer.

468
00:27:45,464 --> 00:27:48,914
The fix comes from NVIDIA's LatentMoE design.

469
00:27:48,914 --> 00:27:53,052
Before dispatching a token, compress it to a latent vector of half the width:

470
00:27:53,052 --> 00:27:55,727
3,584 numbers.

471
00:27:55,727 --> 00:27:59,764
The routed experts work entirely in that smaller space, and their blended

472
00:27:59,764 --> 00:28:02,652
result is projected back up to full width.

473
00:28:02,652 --> 00:28:05,802
The two shared experts still see the full-width vector.

474
00:28:05,802 --> 00:28:10,064
Half the width means half the traffic, and smaller experts, so for the same

475
00:28:10,064 --> 00:28:12,464
budget you can afford more of them.

476
00:28:12,464 --> 00:28:16,989
At this extreme sparsity, Moonshot ran into instabilities, and fixed them

477
00:28:16,989 --> 00:28:18,964
with two small changes.

478
00:28:18,964 --> 00:28:23,702
First, a normalization right before projecting back up, so the routed branch's

479
00:28:23,702 --> 00:28:28,814
scale can't swing around depending on which experts were picked.

480
00:28:28,981 --> 00:28:31,369
Second, a new activation function.

481
00:28:31,369 --> 00:28:34,244
Most modern feed-forward networks use SwiGLU:

482
00:28:34,244 --> 00:28:38,769
one branch goes through a smooth gate, x times the sigmoid of x, and multiplies

483
00:28:38,769 --> 00:28:39,994
the other branch.

484
00:28:39,994 --> 00:28:44,381
Both factors grow without limit, so when two big numbers happen to coincide,

485
00:28:44,381 --> 00:28:46,019
their product explodes.

486
00:28:46,019 --> 00:28:48,744
That's dangerous in low-precision arithmetic.

487
00:28:48,744 --> 00:28:52,419
K3's SiTU-GLU wraps each factor in a soft cap:

488
00:28:52,419 --> 00:28:55,381
beta times tanh of x over beta.

489
00:28:55,381 --> 00:28:57,756
Near zero it behaves just like SwiGLU;

490
00:28:57,756 --> 00:29:01,056
far out, it levels off, here at a hundred.

491
00:29:01,056 --> 00:29:05,831
Altogether, Moonshot reports that K3's new architecture, data, and training

492
00:29:05,831 --> 00:29:10,919
recipe reach the same loss as its predecessor, Kimi K2, with about two and

493
00:29:10,919 --> 00:29:12,631
a half times less compute.

494
00:29:16,913 --> 00:29:18,900
Let's put the three side by side.

495
00:29:18,900 --> 00:29:22,588
Here's how much cache memory each one needs as the context grows to a million

496
00:29:22,588 --> 00:29:25,488
tokens, computed from their configurations.

497
00:29:25,488 --> 00:29:26,163
MiMo:

498
00:29:26,163 --> 00:29:30,663
about 51 gigabytes at a million, almost all of it from its 10 global layers.

499
00:29:30,663 --> 00:29:31,413
GLM:

500
00:29:31,413 --> 00:29:32,813
about 93.

501
00:29:32,813 --> 00:29:35,413
Its sparse attention saves compute, not memory:

502
00:29:35,413 --> 00:29:39,150
every token's latent has to be kept, in case the indexer picks it.

503
00:29:39,150 --> 00:29:39,925
Kimi:

504
00:29:39,925 --> 00:29:44,888
about 28, plus its fixed 0.4 gigabytes of KDA memories.

505
00:29:44,888 --> 00:29:50,088
For comparison, textbook attention at GLM's size would leave this chart almost

506
00:29:50,088 --> 00:29:54,438
immediately, on its way to 5 terabytes.

507
00:29:54,563 --> 00:29:56,600
They also share a trick for speed:

508
00:29:56,600 --> 00:29:58,800
guessing several tokens at once.

509
00:29:58,800 --> 00:30:03,475
Each one has a small extra module that drafts the next few tokens cheaply.

510
00:30:03,475 --> 00:30:07,613
Then the full model checks all the guesses in a single pass, and keeps every

511
00:30:07,613 --> 00:30:10,563
guess it agrees with, up to the first mistake.

512
00:30:10,563 --> 00:30:16,113
MiMo's drafter is five layers deep, and proposes seven tokens at a time.

513
00:30:16,113 --> 00:30:22,400
GLM's reuses the indexer's choices and the main model's cache, which in Z.ai's

514
00:30:22,400 --> 00:30:27,450
tests raised the average number of accepted tokens by 20 percent.

515
00:30:27,450 --> 00:30:32,688
Kimi K3's report describes a single-layer drafter.

516
00:30:32,821 --> 00:30:35,634
And they store their numbers in remarkably few bits.

517
00:30:35,634 --> 00:30:40,034
Here are 32 real weights from one of Kimi K3's experts, exactly as stored

518
00:30:40,034 --> 00:30:41,209
in the file.

519
00:30:41,209 --> 00:30:44,846
Each one takes just four bits, and a four-bit number can only be one of these

520
00:30:44,846 --> 00:30:47,809
fifteen values, from minus six to six.

521
00:30:47,809 --> 00:30:51,696
Every block of 32 weights shares one scale factor, a power of two:

522
00:30:51,696 --> 00:30:53,884
here, two to the minus six.

523
00:30:53,884 --> 00:30:59,571
That's how Kimi K3's 2.8 trillion parameters fit in about one and a half terabytes.

524
00:30:59,571 --> 00:31:03,134
Moonshot trained with this format in the loop, so the model learned to live

525
00:31:03,134 --> 00:31:04,046
with it.

526
00:31:04,046 --> 00:31:06,834
MiMo stores its experts in four bits too;

527
00:31:06,834 --> 00:31:09,871
GLM uses eight.

528
00:31:09,996 --> 00:31:11,821
Here's everything at once.

529
00:31:11,821 --> 00:31:17,021
Three models of broadly similar ambition, all mixtures of experts, all reading

530
00:31:17,021 --> 00:31:21,221
a million tokens, with three different answers to the cost of attention.

531
00:31:23,853 --> 00:31:27,453
So that's the open frontier, as of October 2026.

532
00:31:27,453 --> 00:31:30,391
Underneath, the same skeleton GPT-2 had:

533
00:31:30,391 --> 00:31:34,441
tokens become vectors, attention and feed-forward layers refine them, and

534
00:31:34,441 --> 00:31:37,003
the last vector predicts the next token.

535
00:31:37,003 --> 00:31:39,128
On top of it, two kinds of bets.

536
00:31:39,128 --> 00:31:43,028
On width, all three turned the feed-forward network into a vast library of

537
00:31:43,028 --> 00:31:47,391
experts, with only a few percent of the model at work for any one token.

538
00:31:47,391 --> 00:31:50,178
On length, three different answers.

539
00:31:50,178 --> 00:31:53,678
MiMo looks nearby, and keeps a few global layers.

540
00:31:53,678 --> 00:31:58,578
GLM compresses its memory, and reads only what its indexer picks.

541
00:31:58,578 --> 00:32:03,178
Kimi keeps a running memory, attends globally only every fourth layer, and

542
00:32:03,178 --> 00:32:06,395
